{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "OOP \"Object Oriented Programming\""}, {"type": "position", "content": 10}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to talk about OOP."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Explain important features related to object programming in different contexts and with different types of object programming."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations.   Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n4le01ob10re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Simple Past, Simple Present, and Present Continuous"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use these structures to talk about the development of object oriented programming through history. We are going to review the <strong>simple past,</strong> the <strong>simple present</strong>, and the <strong>present continuous."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<strong>Simple past</strong> is the tense you use to talk about past events, therefore use it when you want to talk about the history of things. Let's take a quick look at the chart which contains the structures and examples in <i>affirmative, negative,</i> and <i>simple questions."}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":3,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Affirmative\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Negative\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Simple Questions\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Structure:</br></br> <i> Subject + verb in simple past + complement.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Structure: </br></br> <i>Subject + did not /didn't + verb in present simple + complement.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Structure: </br></br> <i>Did + subject + verb in simple present + complement + ? \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Example:</br></br>  <i>The first programmers <strong>developed</strong> Simula 67, the first object-oriented language, in the 1960's.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Example:</br></br>  <i>The first programmers <strong>didn't develop</strong> Simula 67, the first object-oriented language, in the 1960's.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Example:</br></br>  <i>Did the programmers <strong>develop</strong> Simula 67, the first object-oriented language, in the 1960's?\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "text", "content": " Remember, there are two main forms of the verbs in simple past, <strong>regular verbs</strong> and <strong>irregular verbs</strong>. You can find lists in books and on the Internet."}, {"type": "text", "content": " <strong>Simple present</strong> is the tense you use to talk about facts, so use it when you want to talk about general facts about something. Let's take a quick look at the chart which contains the structures and examples in <i>affirmative, negative,</i> and <i>simple questions."}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":3,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Affirmative\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Negative\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Simple Questions\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Structure:</br></br>  <i>Subject + verb in present simple + complement</i></br></br><strong>Note:</strong> Is it called <strong>third person rule</strong>, if the sentence use the <strong>third person singular</strong>, you add <i><strong>-s, -es, -ies</i></strong> to the verbs. This rule doesn't apply with the present of the verb to be. \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Structure:</br></br>  <i>Subject + do not or don't/ does not or doesn't + complement.</i></br></br>  Remember, if you have the auxiliary <strong>does</strong> in the sentence, the third person rule doesn't apply.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Structure:</br></br>  <i>Do or Does + subject + verb in simple present + complement + ?</i></br></br>  Remember, if you have the auxiliary <strong>does</strong> in the question, the third person rule doesn't apply.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Example:</br></br>  <i>OOP <strong>possesses</strong> many different features.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Example:</br></br>   <i>OOP <strong>doesn't possess</strong> many different features.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Example:</br></br>   <i><strong>Does</strong> OOP <strong>possess</strong> many different features?\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p><strong>Present continuous</strong> is the tense you use to talk about actions in progress, therefore use it when describing events of something happening at the moment. Let&rsquo;s take a quick look at the chart which contains the structures and examples in <i>affirmative, negative,</i> and <i>simple questions</i>.</p>"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":3,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Affirmative\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Negative\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Simple Questions\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Structure: </br></br>  <i>Subject + verb in simple past + complement.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Structure: </br></br>  <i>Subject + did not /didn't + verb in present simple + complement.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Structure: </br></br>  <i>Did + subject + verb in simple present + complement + ? \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Example: </br></br>  <i>People <strong>are learning</strong> more about object-orienting languages.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Example: </br></br>  <i>People <strong>aren't learning</strong> more about object-orienting languages\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Example: </br></br>   <i><strong>Are</strong> people <strong>learning</strong> more about object-orienting languages?\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I'm reading about the history of OOP."}, {"type": "example", "content": "The author is telling that Object-Oriented Programming or OOP was the new conception of programming in the late 70's."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Its approach, based on the idea that we interact with single objects, it becomes part of a whole, so we cannot mix their attributes and capabilities."}, {"type": "example", "content": "The earliest approaches that programming managed, consisted of an arrangement of variables and data to be included into an algorithm to fulfill the desired results."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Possessive Adjectives: Its - Theirs"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<strong>Possessive adjectives</strong> are words you use to point out the possession in a sentence."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>You use<i> <strong>its</strong></i> to indicate that an object or set of objects refers to something.</p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>This is first <strong>object</strong>, <strong>its</strong> background color is blue.</i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>You use <strong><i>their</i></strong> to indicate that an object or set of objects refers to several things or more than one person.</p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>Those are <strong>the new programmers</strong>, Tom and Clarice, <strong>their</strong> job is easier thanks to OOP.</i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>Note:</strong> You choose to use <strong>its</strong> or <strong>their </strong>depending on the owner, not on the objects.&nbsp;</p> <p>Examples:</p> <p><strong><i>Its</i></strong><i> (the object) color is red.</i></p> <p><strong><i>Their</i></strong><i> (Tom and Clarice) job is easier thanks to OOP.</i></p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Therefore, each piece must have its own independent features."}, {"type": "example", "content": "This was useful for simple programs, but as the complexity increased, it became more problematic to apply this method for any modification, required in its original definitions of data structured."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Computer programmers tried to establish some programming rules to avoid side effects, but their new ways to program were still in process."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "capability "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "capacity, skill"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Its approach becomes part of a whole, so we cannot mix their attributes and capabilities."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le01ob10aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "whole "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the total amount of something"}